## Type system

Never use an escape hatch ({{TYPE_ESCAPE_HATCHES}}) to silence a type error. The
error is telling you a contract does not fit; fix the contract or report
`blocked`.

## Commands for the TDD loop

`tdd` names no commands; it points here. Run every command from `{{WORKDIR}}`.

- **Targeted test run:** `{{TEST_TARGETED}}` — {{TEST_TARGET_HINT}}
- **Gate:** {{GATE}} (steps under "After edits").
- **Red stub:** stated once, for your layer's return shape, in your agent
  file's "Tests are yours" section.

## TDD is not optional

If your layer is tested, follow `tdd` exactly: the failing test first, watched
fail, before production code. `STATUS: done` requires an executed, green run —
never a run you reasoned about instead of executing.

If your layer is not tested, you still never make a tested layer's test pass by
editing that layer's code. Report `STATUS: blocked` and name its owner.

## Boundaries

Before placing any logic, apply `hexagonal-architecture` (is the boundary
respected?) then `separation-of-concerns` (which side does this go on?). Both
failure modes — logic drifting one layer outward, and infrastructure leaking one
layer inward — are invisible to the gate and to the tests. They are only caught
here and by `{{LAYER_REVIEWER}}`.

If the correct placement is outside your `owns` globs, report `STATUS: blocked`
and name the owning agent. Never park logic in your layer because it was the
layer you had open.

## Decomposition

Apply `extract-til-you-drop` to every non-trivial function you write, in the
refactor step of the TDD loop. This is not a refactor-only rule — it applies to
new code.

